
MoM – Azure Migration & Future-State Architecture

Key Discussion Points

* Evaluate 2–3 Azure/Databricks architecture options and compare them on cost, scalability, latency, performance, security, migration effort, and maintainability.
* Review the existing database and code repositories before recommending the future-state architecture.
* Finalize the Supabase replacement, application hosting, database strategy, permissions/authorization, and Blob/File Storage approach.
* Use the Azure migration as an opportunity to identify and consolidate redundant tables/backend functionality, while documenting trade-offs.
* Establish a shared enterprise foundation for common capabilities such as files, comments, notifications, work items, approvals, and agent runs.
* Explore a universal entity graph with polymorphic relationships between entities.
* Support record-level permissions using users/teams + roles such as Viewer, Editor, and Publisher.
* Provide a centralized work/notification experience so users do not need to check multiple applications for pending work.
* Consider the future possibility of replacing Salesforce functionality within the Oil Console, without making it part of the immediate migration scope.
* The first two migrated applications should serve as the “guiding light” / reference architecture for future enterprise applications.
* Current implementation scope remains focused on the original migration, TOAP, and Knowledge Base, while POCs and other application development can continue.

